// F20 -- a multiplicity whose lower bound exceeds its upper bound is accepted, and COMPILES.
//
// Run:
//   java -cp <engine-runner>/target/classes:$(cat cp.txt) perf.ParseMain model.pure --compile
//
// Observed: ok  [compiles]
// Expected: rejected somewhere -- no value can ever satisfy [2..1].
//
// This is not a parse-stage complaint. `[1..]` (open upper bound) IS a grammar error, so the
// multiplicity syntax is validated; what is missing is the range check, and the compiler does
// not add one either. The property is simply unsatisfiable and nothing says so.
//
// Contrast, in the same file: an Association with one property parses and is then rejected at
// COMPILE with "Expected 2 properties for an association". So arity IS checked one stage
// later -- the pattern exists, multiplicity bounds are just not part of it.
//
// Found while verifying a permissiveness report rather than while looking for defects: the
// claim was "reversed multiplicity parses", and it turned out to compile too.
###Pure
Class fx::repro::Impossible
{
   // Lower bound 2, upper bound 1. Accepted through parse AND compile.
   never: String[2..1];
   alsoNever: Integer[10..3];
}
